NUMBER THEORY

Basics

Q1. Find all integers n, such that (n+1)|(n^2+1). 
Q2. Prove that there are infinitely many positive integers n, such that both 5 and 13 divide 4n^2+1.
Q3. Prove that for every positive integer n, there exists a positive integer x, such that n divides each term of the sequence x+1,x^x+1,x^{x^x}+1,\ldots.
Q4.  Find all odd positive integers such that 3|(n2^n+1).
Q5. Prove that for every integer k the numbers 2k+l and 9k+4 are relatively prime, and for numbers 2k-l and' 9k+4 find their greatest common divisor as a function of k.
Q6. Prove that every integer> 6 can be represented as a sum of two integers > 1 which are relatively prime.
Q7. Prove that infinite sequence {2n-3} (n=2,3,4,…) contains infinitely many numbers divisible by either 5 or 13, but there doesn’t exist any no in the sequence divisible by 5∙13.
Q8. A natural number n is chosen strictly between two consecutive perfect squares. The smaller of these two squares is obtained by subtracting k from n and the larger one is obtained by adding l to n. Prove that n − kl is a perfect square.
Q9. Prove that every prime of the form 4k + 1 is a hypotenuse of a right triangle with integer sides.
Q10. Find all primes which can be represented both as sums and as differences of two primes.

