Very Hard

Q1. Start with a positive integer, then choose a negative integer. These numbers are then used to generate a sequence where each term is a sum of the previous two terms.
For example, if we started with 6 and −5, we would get the sequence 6, −5,1,−4, −3,−7,−10, −17,−27,... which starts with 4 elements that alternate sign before the terms are all negative. 
If we started with 3 and −2, we would get the sequence 3, −2, 1,−1, 0, −1,−1,−2,−3,... which also starts with 4 elements that alternate sign before the terms are all non-positive (we don’t count 0 in the alternating part). 
(a) Can you find a sequence of this type that starts with 5 elements that alternate sign? With 10 elements that alternate sign? Can you find a sequence with any number of elements that alternate sign? 
(b) Given a particular starting integer, what negative number should you choose to make the alternating part of the sequence as long as possible? For example, if your sequence started with 8, what negative number would give the longest alternating part? What if you started with 10? With n?
